 In the many, many games I have played I have never been killed once
 by drowning in water, however I think the "pool" aspect could be made
 more "playable"

 I think that your fate, sinking or swiming, should be a function of
 three or four aspects.
	1. Total weight of armor being worn
	2. Total weight of "pack"
	3. Dexterity
	4. Luck 

 The function could be dependent upon some upper limit of weigth
 modified by the characters dexterity and luck; for example:
   A) a clumsy and unlucky adventurer would only be able to shed some
      of the items in his pack...is he light enough to swim?
   B) a average adventurer would be able to shed his entire pack...is 
      he light enough to swim?
   C) a very dexterous and lucky adventer would be able to shed his pack
      and some armor...is he light enough to swim?

 Of course the character should loose all gold, weapon in hand, and
 anything shed or droped while in the pool where it remains at the bottom,
 a high price for survival.
 
 Then again, the general store could sell some SCUBA gear.......

